= 1
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=1}

Use the standard regular self-adjoint assumptions on the <Sturm-Liouville problem>, including positive weight $w$. The normalized <eigenfunctions> obey <orthogonality>
$$
\int_a^bw(x)y_m(x)^*y_n(x)dx=\delta_{mn}.
$$
Completeness means every function in the weighted $L^2$ space has expansion $f(x)=\sum_ny_n(x)\int_a^bw(\xi)y_n(\xi)^*f(\xi)d\xi$, with convergence in that space. Comparing the action of this kernel on test functions with the <Dirac delta> gives, in the distributional sense,
$$
\boxed{w(\xi)\sum_{n=1}^\infty y_n(\xi)^*y_n(x)=\delta(x-\xi).}
$$
If zero is not an <eigenvalue>, divide each term by $\lambda_n$. Since $Ly_n=\lambda_ny_n$, application of $L$ recovers the displayed delta expansion, proving
$$
\boxed{G(x;\xi)=w(\xi)\sum_{n=1}^\infty\frac{y_n(x)y_n(\xi)^*}{\lambda_n}.}
$$
The factor $w(\xi)$ is required because the source equation uses an ordinary, unweighted delta. It distinguishes the <normalized Sturm-Liouville spectral Green kernel> from a kernel integrated against weighted source measure. If there is a zero <eigenvalue>, an inverse for arbitrary forcing does not exist; the unqualified division by that <eigenvalue> is invalid.

For $L=-d^2/dx^2+\omega^2$ with real $\omega$ and zero endpoints on $[0,1]$, the weight is one, $y_n=\sqrt2\sin(n\pi x)$ and $\lambda_n=n^2\pi^2+\omega^2>0$. Substitution yields
$$
\boxed{G(x;\xi)=\sum_{n=1}^\infty\frac{2\sin(n\pi\xi)\sin(n\pi x)}{n^2\pi^2+\omega^2}.}
$$
The series is absolutely and uniformly convergent, although applying the differential operator is understood distributionally at its source point.

= 2
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2}

Set $\Phi=R(r)P(\theta)$ and multiply the <Laplace equation> by $r^2/(RP)$. <Separation of variables> gives
$$
\frac{(r^2R')'}R=-\frac{(\sin\theta P')'}{\sin\theta P}=\lambda.
$$
With $\xi=\cos\theta$, the angular equation is $[(1-\xi^2)P_\xi]' +\lambda P=0$. Regularity at both poles selects the <Legendre polynomials> $P_n$ and $\lambda=n(n+1)$. The radial <Cauchy-Euler equation> has solutions $r^n,r^{-n-1}$. Expanding the angular data in this complete orthogonal system therefore gives
$$
\Phi(r,\theta)=\sum_{n=0}^\infty(A_nr^n+B_nr^{-n-1})P_n(\cos\theta),
$$
with the usual convergence required of a <harmonic> expansion on the region considered. Angular Legendre functions singular at the poles are excluded.

For $0<a<b$, the outer data have expansion $b^2\cos^2\theta=b^2/3+(2b^2/3)P_2(\cos\theta)$, while the inner data are pure $P_0$. Thus only degrees zero and two are needed. A radial interpolation matching each pair of boundary values gives
$$
\boxed{\Phi(r,\theta)=\frac{a^3(b-r)+(b^3/3)(r-a)}{r(b-a)}
+\frac{2b^2}{3}\frac{r^2-a^5/r^3}{b^2-a^5/b^3}P_2(\cos\theta).}
$$
The first term is a <linear combination> of $1,r^{-1}$ and takes values $a^2,b^2/3$ at the two radii. The second is a combination of $r^2,r^{-3}$ and takes values zero and $2b^2/3$. These checks establish both boundary conditions; each term is <harmonic>. The <maximum principle for harmonic functions> gives uniqueness. This is an example of <axisymmetric harmonic interpolation between concentric spheres>.

= 3
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=3}

Write $R=|x-x_0|$. A radial function <harmonic> away from the source satisfies $(R^2G')'=0$, hence $G=A+B/R$. Decay at infinity forces $A=0$. Integrating $\Delta G=\delta(x-x_0)$ over a small sphere, the <divergence theorem> requires flux one:
$$
4\pi\epsilon^2G'(\epsilon)=-4\pi B=1.
$$
Therefore the <fundamental solution> in this sign convention is
$$
\boxed{G_0(x,x_0)=-\frac1{4\pi|x-x_0|}.}
$$
Adding point sources outside the domain changes only a <harmonic function> inside it. The <method of images> selects their positions and strengths to cancel boundary values. The sign reverses for the alternative convention $-\Delta G=\delta$.

= i
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Let the source be $x_0=(a,b,c)$ with all three coordinates positive. Reflect it independently in the three coordinate planes: the eight points are $x_\epsilon=(\epsilon_1a,\epsilon_2b,\epsilon_3c)$, with $\epsilon_j\in\{1,-1\}$. Assign strength $s_\epsilon=\epsilon_1\epsilon_2\epsilon_3$. Then
$$
\boxed{G(x,x_0)=-\frac1{4\pi}\sum_{\epsilon\in\{\pm1\}^3}\frac{\epsilon_1\epsilon_2\epsilon_3}{|x-x_\epsilon|},\qquad N=7.}
$$
The all-positive point is the original source. On any boundary plane, two terms differing only in that plane's sign have equal distances and opposite strengths, so cancel. All seven additional points lie outside the positive octant, leaving exactly the required interior source. This is the octant <Dirichlet Green function>.

= ii
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Let $r_0=|x_0|$ with $0<r_0<a$. The <Dirichlet Green function inside a sphere> uses the exterior inverse point $x_*=a^2x_0/r_0^2$ and strength $s_*=-a/r_0$. Thus
$$
\boxed{G(x,x_0)=-\frac1{4\pi}\left[\frac1{|x-x_0|}-\frac{a/r_0}{|x-x_*|}\right],\quad x_*=\frac{a^2x_0}{r_0^2},\quad N=1.}
$$
For $|x|=a$, the distance identity $|x-x_*|=(a/r_0)|x-x_0|$ makes the bracket zero. The image is outside the sphere, so it adds no interior singularity. This is the radial weight associated with the <Kelvin transform>.

**The printed unit-strength restriction must be relaxed in this part:** the required single image has strength $-a/r_0$, whose magnitude is generally not one. A reflected unit charge does not give the spherical boundary cancellation. For a centered source, the limiting formula is
$$
\boxed{G(x,0)=-\frac1{4\pi|x|}+\frac1{4\pi a}.}
$$
Its correcting term is constant and corresponds to the weighted image moving to infinity, rather than a finite unit point image.

= iii
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Take the source at the origin and write $\rho^2=y^2+z^2$. Repeated reflection in $x=\pm1$ places the sources at $(2n,0,0)$ with strengths $(-1)^n$, $n\in\mathbb Z$. There are **countably infinitely many images**; $n=0$ is the original source. A convergent prescription groups adjacent opposite charges:
$$
\boxed{G(x,y,z;0)=-\frac1{4\pi}\sum_{m\in\mathbb Z}\left[\frac1{\sqrt{(x-4m)^2+\rho^2}}-\frac1{\sqrt{(x-4m-2)^2+\rho^2}}\right].}
$$
Each bracket is $O(|m|^{-2})$ on compact sets away from the source, so the paired series converges absolutely. The individual unsigned point-charge terms would not be absolutely summable. On $x=1$, reflection pairs the index $n$ with $1-n$; on $x=-1$, it pairs $n$ with $-1-n$. Their distances agree and signs oppose. Symmetric finite cutoffs leave only a vanishing far-tail contribution, proving both zero boundary values. All nonzero-index sources lie outside the slab. This is the centered-source version of the <Dirichlet Green function between parallel planes>, selected to decay at large transverse distance.

