{title=Image locations and strengths for an octant, an interior spherical source and two parallel plates}

= 4
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=4}

<Cauchy's integral theorem> states that a <holomorphic function> on a simply connected domain has zero <contour integral> around every closed piecewise smooth curve in that domain. One proof begins with the <Cauchy theorem for a triangle>. Subdivide a triangle into four equal triangles. Their oriented boundary integrals sum to the original integral because internal edges cancel, so choose a nested subtriangle carrying at least one quarter of the preceding integral's modulus. At stage $n$, its perimeter and diameter are $P/2^n,d/2^n$, and the nested triangles converge to a point $z_0$. Complex differentiability gives $f(z)=f(z_0)+f'(z_0)(z-z_0)+\epsilon(z)(z-z_0)$, with $\epsilon(z)\to0$. The affine terms have primitives and integrate to zero. Therefore $|I_n|\leq\sup|\epsilon|\,Pd/4^n$, while $|I_0|\leq4^n|I_n|$. Letting $n\to\infty$ proves $I_0=0$. Triangulation cancels internal edges for polygonal contours; local primitives and deformation through the domain extend the result to the stated piecewise smooth curves. Simple connectedness ensures the necessary contractions do not pass through a missing point.

Apply the theorem to $e^{-az^2}$ on the rectangle between heights zero and $\rho$, with vertical sides at $\operatorname{Re}z=\pm R$. On either vertical side,
$$
|e^{-az^2}|=e^{-aR^2+a(\operatorname{Im}z)^2}\leq e^{-aR^2+a\rho^2}.
$$
The modulus of each side integral is at most $|\rho|e^{-aR^2+a\rho^2}\to0$. Both horizontal integrals converge absolutely. The two horizontal orientations are opposite, so the limit gives $I(\rho,a)=I(0,a)$, also for negative $\rho$. This is <Gaussian contour translation>.

Now complete the square:
$$
-ax^2+i\nu x=-a\left(x-\frac{i\nu}{2a}\right)^2-\frac{\nu^2}{4a}.
$$
The substitution $z=x-i\nu/(2a)$ moves the path to height $-\nu/(2a)$. Its Gaussian integral equals the real-axis integral by the preceding result. Hence
$$
\boxed{\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}e^{-ax^2+i\nu x}dx=\sqrt{\frac\pi a}\,e^{-\nu^2/(4a)},\qquad a>0,\ \nu\in\mathbb R.}
$$
This also gives the <Fourier transform of a Gaussian> in the convention with no prefactor in the forward transform.

= 5
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=5}

Treat the left side as a causal <convolution> with $t^{-\alpha}$. Under the usual Laplace-transform hypotheses, the <convolution theorem for Laplace transforms> gives
$$
\widehat\Phi(s)\,\Gamma(1-\alpha)s^{\alpha-1}=\widehat f(s).
$$
For an absolutely continuous $f$ with $f(0)=0$, the <derivative> transform is $\mathcal L[f']=s\widehat f$. Consequently
$$
\widehat\Phi(s)=\frac{s^{-\alpha}}{\Gamma(1-\alpha)}\mathcal L[f'](s).
$$
Since $\mathcal L[t^{\alpha-1}]=\Gamma(\alpha)s^{-\alpha}$, inverting the product proves
$$
\boxed{\Phi(t)=\frac1{\Gamma(\alpha)\Gamma(1-\alpha)}\int_0^tf'(\tau)(t-\tau)^{\alpha-1}d\tau.}
$$
Here $0<\alpha<1$ makes both power kernels locally integrable. For example assume exponential growth bounds sufficient for the transforms and a regularity class where the <convolution> is defined. This is the <Abel integral equation of fractional order>; the initial value removes the extra boundary term in the <derivative> transform.

For $f(t)=t$, the remaining integral is $\int_0^t(t-\tau)^{\alpha-1}d\tau=t^\alpha/\alpha$. Thus
$$
\boxed{\Phi(t)=\frac{t^\alpha}{\Gamma(\alpha+1)\Gamma(1-\alpha)}.}
$$
Substitution into the original equation checks the result: scaling $\tau=tu$ gives $t\,B(\alpha+1,1-\alpha)/[\Gamma(\alpha+1)\Gamma(1-\alpha)]=t$ by the <beta-gamma identity>.

= 6
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=6}

An <isotropic tensor> has unchanged components under every proper rotation of the Cartesian axes. Every rank-zero <scalar> is isotropic, and $c\delta_{ij}$ is a rank-two example. The zero rank-one tensor is isotropic, but no nonzero <vector> is: a half-turn about an axis perpendicular to it reverses its sign.

For the <inertia tensor>, write $u=x-G$ and $X=P-G$, so $x-P=u-X$. Expand its defining integrand. Every cross term has an integral containing $\int_Bu\rho\,d^3x=0$ by the definition of the <centre of mass>. The remaining terms give the <tensor parallel-axis theorem>:
$$
\boxed{I_{ij}(P)-I_{ij}(G)=M(|X|^2\delta_{ij}-X_iX_j).}
$$
For a uniform unit ball, rotational symmetry gives $\int\rho u_iu_jd^3u=c\delta_{ij}$. Its trace is $\int\rho|u|^2d^3u=3M/5$, so $c=M/5$ and $I(G)=2MI/5$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{I(P)=M\left[\left(\frac25+|X|^2\right)I-XX^T\right].}
$$
Assuming positive mass, it is isotropic exactly when **$P=G$**. For $X\ne0$, its <eigenvalues> and directions are
$$
\boxed{\lambda_\parallel=\frac{2M}{5},\quad e_\parallel=X/|X|;\qquad
\lambda_\perp=M\left(\frac25+|X|^2\right)\quad\text{on }X^\perp.}
$$
To give an explicit orthonormal <eigenbasis> when $X_1^2+X_2^2>0$, take $e_1=(-X_2,X_1,0)/\sqrt{X_1^2+X_2^2}$ and $e_2=e_\parallel\times e_1$. If $X$ is a nonzero <vector> along the third axis, take the first two coordinate <unit vectors> instead. At $X=0$, all three <eigenvalues> are $2M/5$ and every <orthonormal basis> works. The <inertia tensor of a displaced uniform ball> describes the same result for an arbitrary radius.

= 7
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=7}

The quadratic <Lagrangian> gives $\partial L/\partial\dot q=T\dot q$ and $\partial L/\partial q=-Vq$. Thus the <Euler-Lagrange equations> are
$$
T\ddot q+Vq=0.
$$
A nonzero mode <vector> $v$ has a common <harmonic> time factor and satisfies the <generalized eigenvalue problem for small oscillations> $Vv=\omega^2Tv$; $\omega$ is its <normal frequency>. A zero-frequency mode instead admits a constant or linear time factor.

Measure the three coordinates as displacements from a configuration with both springs unstretched. The <kinetic energy> is $\tfrac12\dot x_1^2+\tfrac12M\dot x_2^2+\tfrac12\dot x_3^2$, and the <potential energy> is $\tfrac12(x_2-x_1)^2+\tfrac12(x_3-x_2)^2$. Expansion gives
$$
T=\begin{pmatrix}1&0&0\\0&M&0\\0&0&1\end{pmatrix},\qquad
V=\begin{pmatrix}1&-1&0\\-1&2&-1\\0&-1&1\end{pmatrix}.
$$
For $M>0$, direct substitution yields the three <normal modes>:
$$
\boxed{\begin{array}{c|c|c}
&v&\omega\\\hline
\text{translation}&(1,1,1)^T&0\\
\text{antisymmetric outer motion}&(1,0,-1)^T&1\\
\text{symmetric outer motion}&(1,-2/M,1)^T&\sqrt{1+2/M}
\end{array}.}
$$
The three <vectors> are independent and orthogonal in the mass inner product $v^TTw$, so they exhaust the motion. Their squared mass norms are $M+2,2,2+4/M$; division by the corresponding square roots gives normalized modes. This is the unit-spring, unit-outer-mass case of the <normal modes of three masses connected by two equal springs>.

As $M\to\infty$, the central displacement in the symmetric oscillatory mode tends to zero and both nonzero frequencies tend to one. The two limiting shapes $(1,0,1)$ and $(1,0,-1)$ span independent motions of the outer masses against an effectively fixed centre; each unit mass has its own unit spring frequency. The translational mode remains at zero frequency. Under a bounded kinetic-energy condition its common velocity tends to zero as $M^{-1/2}$, while a static common displacement is still an equilibrium shift. One must specify that energy condition before concluding that an arbitrary freely translating heavy mass remains stationary.

\Image[paper-2-three-mass-normal-modes.png]
{title=Mode shapes of a free three-mass chain, including the zero-frequency translation}

= 8
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=8}

A <group homomorphism> preserves multiplication: $\Phi(gh)=\Phi(g)\Phi(h)$. Its <group kernel> $K=\{g:\Phi(g)=e_H\}$ is a subgroup, and $\Phi(gkg^{-1})=e_H$ for $k\in K$. Thus $gKg^{-1}=K$, proving it is a <normal subgroup>. The map $gK\mapsto\Phi(g)$ is well-defined because two representatives of a coset differ by an element of $K$. It is a homomorphism, has trivial kernel, and is surjective when $\Phi$ is. This proves the <first isomorphism theorem>, $G/K\cong H$. This proof does not use finiteness and therefore also applies below.

The identity <matrix> belongs to $SL_2(\mathbb C)$. The <determinant> is multiplicative, giving closure, and <matrix> multiplication is associative. The inverse of a determinant-one <matrix> $A=\begin{pmatrix}a&b\\c&d\end{pmatrix}$ is $\begin{pmatrix}d&-b\\-c&a\end{pmatrix}$, also of determinant one. Thus this <special linear group> is a group.

The fractional linear maps must be interpreted on the <Riemann sphere>, including infinity: a finite pole is sent to infinity and infinity is sent to $a/c$ when $c\ne0$. As literal everywhere-defined maps of the complex plane they would not form the asserted group. Substitution into the two rational expressions gives $f_A\circ f_B=f_{AB}$. Matrix identities and inverses therefore give the identity and inverse transformations, proving that the <Möbius group> is a group and that $\Phi:A\mapsto f_A$ is a surjective homomorphism.

For $f_A$ to be the identity, $(az+b)/(cz+d)=z$ on all finite points where defined. Hence $cz^2+(d-a)z-b$ is the zero polynomial, so $b=c=0$, $d=a$, and $a^2=1$ from the determinant. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\ker\Phi=\{I,-I\},\qquad\mathcal M\cong SL_2(\mathbb C)/\{\pm I\}=PSL_2(\mathbb C).}
$$
The kernel is central and hence normal. In particular $\Phi$ is **not an <isomorphism>**, since $A$ and $-A$ represent the same transformation. The quotient is the <projective special linear group> in this complex two-dimensional case.

= 9
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=9}

For a subgroup $H$ of a <finite group> $G$, distinct left <cosets> are disjoint and partition $G$. Each coset $gH$ has exactly $|H|$ elements because $h\mapsto gh$ is a bijection. Counting the partition proves <Lagrange's theorem>:
$$
\boxed{|G|=[G:H]|H|.}
$$
A <cyclic group> is generated by a single element. In a group of order four, an element of order four generates the whole group, giving $C_4$. Otherwise all three nonidentity elements have order two. Choose two of them $a,b$; cancellation makes $ab=c$ the third. Since $c^{-1}=c$, $(ab)^{-1}=b^{-1}a^{-1}=ba=ab$. The remaining products then give the <Klein four-group>. This proves that **there are precisely two groups of order four up to <isomorphism>**, distinguished by whether an element of order four exists.

For the square, take $r$ to be counterclockwise rotation by $90^\circ$ and $s$ reflection in the horizontal axis. They satisfy $r^4=s^2=e$ and $sr=r^{-1}s$. The <dihedral group> here is $D_4$ of order eight, with elements $e,r,r^2,r^3,s,rs,r^2s,r^3s$. Both generators are necessary: one generator would give a cyclic, hence abelian, group, while $r$ and $s$ do not commute. In the following <Cayley table>, the row acts after the column; the formula $(r^as^b)(r^cs^d)=r^{a+(-1)^bc}s^{b+d}$, with exponents reduced modulo four and two, computes every entry.
$$
\begin{array}{c|cccccccc}
 &e&r&r^2&r^3&s&rs&r^2s&r^3s\\\hline
e&e&r&r^2&r^3&s&rs&r^2s&r^3s\\
r&r&r^2&r^3&e&rs&r^2s&r^3s&s\\
r^2&r^2&r^3&e&r&r^2s&r^3s&s&rs\\
r^3&r^3&e&r&r^2&r^3s&s&rs&r^2s\\
s&s&r^3s&r^2s&rs&e&r^3&r^2&r\\
rs&rs&s&r^3s&r^2s&r&e&r^3&r^2\\
r^2s&r^2s&rs&s&r^3s&r^2&r&e&r^3\\
r^3s&r^3s&r^2s&rs&s&r^3&r^2&r&e\\
\end{array}.
$$
The largest <cyclic subgroup> is $\langle r\rangle=\{e,r,r^2,r^3\}$, of order four. Every reflection has order two and there is no element of order eight. The other subgroups of order four are
$$
\boxed{H_0=\{e,r^2,s,r^2s\},\qquad H_1=\{e,r^2,rs,r^3s\}.}
$$
Both are Klein four-groups, generated by the commuting involutions $r^2$ and one reflection. For completeness, any order-four subgroup containing $r$ or $r^3$ equals $\langle r\rangle$. Otherwise it contains a reflection $f$; the product of two distinct reflections is a nonidentity rotation, which must be $r^2$ if odd powers of $r$ are excluded. This forces the subgroup to be $\{e,r^2,f,r^2f\}$, giving exactly the two displayed choices. It is the square case of the <classification of subgroups of a dihedral group>.

= 10
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=10}

The <Klein four-group> has multiplication table
$$
\begin{array}{c|cccc}
 &1&a&b&c\\\hline
1&1&a&b&c\\
a&a&1&c&b\\
b&b&c&1&a\\
c&c&b&a&1
\end{array}.
$$
Since it is abelian, every <conjugacy class> is a singleton: $\{1\},\{a\},\{b\},\{c\}$. An <irreducible representation> is a nonzero representation whose only invariant subspaces are zero and the whole representation space. For a <finite group>, the number of inequivalent complex irreducible representations equals the number of conjugacy classes, so there are four here.

A one-dimensional representation sends the involutions $a,b$ to signs, independently. All four choices are
$$
\chi_{pq}(1)=1,\quad\chi_{pq}(a)=(-1)^p,\quad\chi_{pq}(b)=(-1)^q,\quad\chi_{pq}(c)=(-1)^{p+q},\qquad p,q\in\{0,1\}.
$$
They are distinct irreducible representations, exhausting the four possibilities. The <character table of the Klein four-group> is
$$
\boxed{\begin{array}{c|rrrr}
 &1&a&b&c\\\hline
\chi_{00}&1&1&1&1\\
\chi_{01}&1&1&-1&-1\\
\chi_{10}&1&-1&1&-1\\
\chi_{11}&1&-1&-1&1
\end{array}.}
$$
The specified diagonal <matrices> obey $D(a)^2=D(b)^2=I$, commute, and satisfy $D(a)D(b)=D(c)$, so the group relations prove that $D$ is a <group representation>. Its coordinate axes are invariant lines carrying respectively $\chi_{01},\chi_{10},\chi_{11}$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{D\cong\chi_{01}\oplus\chi_{10}\oplus\chi_{11}.}
$$
The <trivial representation> $\chi_{00}$ is absent. If $H$ denotes the four-row character <matrix> above, direct row products give $HH^T=4I$. This verifies <character orthogonality>:
$$
\frac14\sum_{g\in V}\overline{\chi_{pq}(g)}\chi_{rs}(g)=\delta_{pr}\delta_{qs}.
$$
The character of $D$ is $(3,-1,-1,-1)$, whose inner products with the four rows are $0,1,1,1$, independently confirming the decomposition. All these irreducibles are real as well: commuting real involutions can be simultaneously diagonalized into their real sign eigenspaces.
